 really well established Trail and it  came down the hill but then it had kind  of a curve to it and you had to you had  to be careful because if you went off  that curve you're going right into a  bunch of bushes and maybe even hitting a  tree and you could go fast down that  hill so we did a lot of that  and we also  um  we took a wagon now like a kid's wagon  one of those red wagons that you see  and we would ride that down Hills we put  like a cushion in it didn't have any  shock absorbers or anything like that I  mean it was like going on a roller  coaster ride basically we're really  lucky we didn't get hurt more than more  often than we did or more seriously than  we did and we would do this down you  know on the roads too we would ride the  wagon we'd go up to one of the big hills  and ride the wagon down the road and he  would steer using the handle on the  wagon that's not a good steering wheel  right it turns the two front wheels and  you could easily flip the whole thing  over well we would do this going down  the hill behind my house which was just  dirt and rocks and wood and grass right  so you get at the top of the hill and  sometimes we'd have like two kids in the  wagon as well really really stupid no  helmets no nothing right and you would  just uh you know the you didn't even  need like to push off gravity itself  would suck you right down and so we did  a lot of a lot of that as a matter of  fact I want to go back to the sledding  as well  so there were all these barbed wire  fences that were you know a legacy of  the farming days and you had to be kind  of careful not to run into them because  you know sometimes they they would be  still still there and one of the hills  I don't know why we did this and why we  didn't just like take the fences down or  something you had to be careful to make  sure you would break before you would  get to the barbed wire fence otherwise  you would hit the barbed wire fence  while you were sledding and you know we  could have cut ourselves up pretty bad  but we we you know we just yeah just  don't hit it it's kind of funny the way  that we were so lackadaisical about  safety stuff so anyway we would uh do  all of these cool things there was also  like a massive rope swing somebody I  don't know who it was I think it might  have been Tony scafito's dad there's a  massive massive Oak uh behind our place  and the scaffitos lived right next door  to us  so anyway there was a rope and it had to  be at least an inch thick that was uh  thrown up oh way way way high over one  of the big thick branches and then tied  off and so we could we could take that  we would run and grab that rope and then  swing way up you know like 10 12 feet in  the air and then come back down you know  and swing back the other way sometimes  we would grab it and swing up and then  let go and go flying and then you know  hit the ground uh somewhere and you know  hopefully not not break a leg or  something like that  um and that was that was a lot of fun as  well we did a lot of great stuff we'd  have picnics out in the woods you know  and when we had a dog too well actually  all the the neighbors dogs loved going  out in the woods it was a lot of fun and  you know we'd hike around and you know  explore and make new trails of our own  and you know sometimes we'd pretend that  we were pirates or  um  that we were in Battlestar Galactica or  Star Wars or that we are Knights on some  sort of mission I mean we would just use  our imagination about what it is that we  were doing and then of course as we got  older we started you know it started  turning into something where our  juvenile delinquency became something  you know we we started uh pretending to  smoke we would steal matches and then  take the straw the straw you could like  you know suck air through and we would  light it up and pretend like we were  smoking and you know  um we'd cool off and all that sort of  stuff I remember uh you know  um when we started to discover swear  words we'd go out in the woods and swear  at each other and stuff like that and of  course there was you know some some uh  you could say drinking and fooling  around and stuff like that as we got  older and older because I I lived there  until I was about uh 17 or so and I you  know we we knew these woods and that  area sort of like the back of our hands  and it's interesting because  um  at first there was no Road in the  Kickapoo Trails behind us and then they  made a road and the road went past that  rock pile and we kind of didn't take  much notice of it because only one  Family actually moved in for a while in  the wooded part at the very end of the  road and then another family moved in on  this High Hill area and we're like oh  things are starting to kind of fill in  there but our own subdivision was by the  1980s becoming more and more packed with  uh people as all the Lots sold and  people built houses and there were more  and more kids and you know like an  entire neighborhood uh and you could say  like ecosystem right  clicks could form people uh had their  their people that they liked and they  didn't like and all of that and you know  that area behind our houses was still  the the Woodland the meadow that we  could play in and for a while even uh  there was water like one year super  heavy rains and the backs of a lot of  our yards filled up with water and some  people actually like caught fish and put  the little fish in there until  unfortunately it dried up and the fish  died  um but you know this was like an area  for all of us neighborhood kids to  continually explore and redevelop and  play in and do all sorts of fun cool  stuff and I guess you know kept us out  of our parents hair they kind of knew  more or less where we were although we  were ranging over a several square mile  area where you could have you could say  it was like a a giant Park that we all  had access to that was completely  unofficial and really just the product  of Farmland going fallow  so you know this was uh you know for a  long time a really nice  perk of living where we did uh this un  unused land it wasn't unclaimed of  course because people did own it and  then it started to fill in by the time  that I was you know  17 and we moved out much of the Kickapoo  Trails had been turned into Lots which  people had put houses on the roads had  expanded you couldn't walk anymore all  the way from our place to the Kickapoo  trails and subsequently to that they  actually filled in even more stuff they  cut the they cut all the trees down and  uh you know leveled off the top of the  the hills and put houses up there so now  all of that is is uh quite different  than it used to be there are still some  patches along the road where you can see  the grasses there are still some trees  here and there but it's definitely not  what it used to be and you know I do  remember my dad actually before he died  he he died very young in 1982  when I was 11.  um so you know we'd only been there  since I think 1974 early 1975. he was  already looking for uh a place to build  a new house that would be kind of out in  the middle of nowhere without so many  neighbors I don't think that he was he  was quite so big on the subdivision life  which is interesting because he grew up  in a subdivision in the suburbs of  Chicago so maybe he was trying to get  away from that but  um this was a really important part of  my life I was really fortunate to grow  up in a place where you know it was  relatively safe I mean there were like  you know you know we'll keep an eye out  sometimes for wandering dogs at certain  times and of course you know we could  easily injure ourselves in a variety of  different ways but for the most part it  was uh it was pretty safe and  um we had you know sort of very  extensive experience in Woodcraft and  you know being able to figure out what  to to do all day in the middle of nature  so like you know when we went on camping  trips or something it wasn't really that  big of a transition compared to kids who  you know were from the town or from  cities or stuff like that and and I  remember it very fondly it was a great  uh time and way to to have a childhood  and slowly grow up I was very fortunate  to have the opportunity to do that so I  thought I would uh tell you a little bit  about that and share it with you and  maybe some of you had similar  experiences or maybe you didn't but that  was a very important part of my life  that I I carry around with me everywhere  I go in my memories
 

